James <james.kanze@gmail.com> wrote:
>Independently of the other issues, this raises a point I've been
>vaguely wondering about.  As far as I know, nethack uses the C
>library rand() functions (or perhaps random() or some such on
>platforms where it is available).

Nethack has several options for which RNG it uses, and at least some
versions of Nethack actually *shipped* with a copy of the Berkeley
random() in the source archive because so many Unix systems had the
canonical lame crippled^W^W linear congruential RNG as rand(). (Linux's
libc uses Berkeley random() as rand(), since the C standard makes no
direct requirements of the algorithm used by rand().)

(If you wawnt to see why LCRNGs are useless for this sort of thing, see
   http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_congruential_generator
and its pretty picture of how triples of consecutive results get
distributed.)

Really, it would make most sense to just drag in the Mersenne Twister
and have done with it; the game will have identical randomness on all
platforms. (Crawl did this and it fixed a severe problem with item
generation on the Windows version which led to gold being cripplingly
rare outside of the Orcish Mines.)
